q('A transfusion recipient develops rapid wheeze, hypotension and angioedema. What is the immediate treatment principle?', 'Stop the transfusion and treat anaphylaxis promptly with intramuscular adrenaline and airway/circulatory support',[
 ('Antihistamine alone is sufficient for airway and circulatory compromise','Adrenaline is first-line for anaphylaxis.'),('Continue the same component while arranging an outpatient allergy test','This delays urgent treatment.'),('Diuretic treatment alone','This does not treat anaphylaxis.')],
 'Recognize airway, breathing or circulation involvement early', '**Anaphylaxis requires immediate adrenaline according to the emergency protocol.**',['Give oxygen and appropriate fluid resuscitation.','Escalate airway and critical-care support when needed.'], '**Investigate the cause after stabilization.**',['Tryptase and selected IgA/anti-IgA testing may help in appropriate cases.','Future component planning is individualized with transfusion medicine.'],[['Immediate','Stop and treat anaphylaxis'],['First-line drug','IM adrenaline'],['Later','Cause assessment and prevention plan']],[AN],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has recurrent severe allergic transfusion reactions associated with confirmed severe IgA deficiency and anti-IgA. Which RBC modification may reduce recurrence?', 'Appropriately washed RBCs to reduce residual plasma proteins',[
 ('Irradiation alone','It targets viable lymphocytes rather than removing plasma proteins.'),('Leukoreduction alone as guaranteed removal of IgA','It does not adequately remove soluble plasma proteins.'),('Warming the unit as the specific prevention','Warming does not remove the relevant proteins.')],
 'Component modifications solve different biological problems', '**Washing removes most residual plasma and its proteins.**',['Specialized washing may be needed to achieve sufficiently low IgA.','Plasma-containing products may require an IgA-deficient donor strategy.'], '**Coordinate availability before elective transfusion.**',['Do not delay life-saving emergency transfusion when specialized products are unavailable.','Provide direct observation and capacity to treat anaphylaxis in that situation.'],[['Washing','Plasma-protein reduction'],['Irradiation','Lymphocyte inactivation'],['Emergency','Balance urgency against reaction risk']],[MO,AN],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which component treatment prevents transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease in susceptible recipients?', 'Appropriate irradiation of cellular components, or a validated equivalent pathogen-reduction approach where applicable',[
 ('Routine leukoreduction as a reliable substitute in every case','Residual viable T cells can still cause TA-GVHD.'),('ABO matching alone','It does not inactivate donor T lymphocytes.'),('Washing alone','It does not reliably prevent donor T-cell proliferation.')],
 'Viable donor T cells cause a distinctive and often fatal complication', '**Irradiation prevents lymphocyte proliferation.**',['Indications depend on the recipient, therapy and donor relationship.','Related-donor cellular components require particular attention.'], '**Document the requirement across care settings.**',['Irradiation does not replace compatibility or infection testing.','Some pathogen-reduced platelet systems meet the relevant prevention requirement.'],[['Target','Viable donor T cells'],['Prevention','Irradiation or validated applicable alternative'],['Not sufficient','Leukoreduction alone']],[MO])
q('Which benefit is most directly associated with prestorage leukoreduction?', 'Reduced leukocyte-associated febrile reactions and reduced HLA alloimmunization risk',[
 ('Guaranteed prevention of TA-GVHD without any other modification','It does not reliably remove all viable T cells.'),('Removal of every soluble plasma allergen','That is not its mechanism.'),('Elimination of all bacterial and viral transmission','Leukoreduction does not sterilize a component.')],
 'Removing leukocytes reduces selected transfusion complications', '**Leukoreduction lowers residual white-cell exposure.**',['It also reduces leukocyte-associated CMV transmission risk.','Special populations may have additional CMV-related component requirements.'], '**Match the modification to the indication.**',['Washed and irradiated components serve different purposes.','No single modification eliminates every transfusion risk.'],[['Leukoreduction','White-cell reduction'],['Washing','Plasma reduction'],['Irradiation','T-cell proliferation prevention']],[MO,PL])
q('A patient repeatedly has poor 10–60-minute platelet increments after transfusion. What is the best evaluation approach?', 'Confirm refractoriness with appropriately timed increments/CCI and assess nonimmune causes before targeted HLA/HPA investigation',[
 ('Diagnose HLA alloimmunization from one next-day count','Consumption and timing can explain an inadequate count.'),('Assume a higher ABO antibody titer is the only possible cause','There are several immune and nonimmune mechanisms.'),('Stop all platelet support permanently without assessing bleeding','Support may still be needed while the mechanism is investigated.')],
 'Poor platelet recovery is not synonymous with alloimmunization', '**Sepsis, DIC, fever and splenic sequestration can reduce increments.**',['Obtain appropriately timed counts after sequential suitable transfusions.','CCI adjusts the increment for body size and platelet dose.'], '**If an immune mechanism is supported, select compatible platelets.**',['HLA-selected or crossmatch-compatible units may help.','HPA antibodies are relevant in selected cases.'],[['Early increment','Immediate recovery assessment'],['Nonimmune','Consumption or sequestration'],['Immune','HLA/HPA-directed selection']],[PL],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which electrolyte abnormality can result directly from citrate binding during rapid massive transfusion?', 'Ionized hypocalcemia',[
 ('Hypercalcemia from citrate chelation','Chelation reduces available ionized calcium.'),('Hypermagnesemia as the obligatory primary citrate effect','Citrate can also bind magnesium; this is not the characteristic answer.'),('Hypernatremia as the defining mechanism of citrate toxicity','The critical chelation effect is on divalent ions.')],
 'Metabolic support is part of hemorrhage resuscitation', '**Citrate in blood components binds ionized calcium.**',['Rapid delivery can exceed citrate metabolism, especially in shock.','Low ionized calcium can impair myocardial and coagulation function.'], '**Monitor and correct ionized calcium within the hemorrhage protocol.**',['Also assess temperature, potassium, acid-base state and fibrinogen.','Do not rely on total calcium alone during rapidly changing resuscitation.'],[['Citrate','Binds ionized calcium'],['Risk','Rapid transfusion and impaired metabolism'],['Monitoring','Ionized calcium and wider resuscitation parameters']],[MA])
q('Which combination can complicate rapid large-volume RBC transfusion?', 'Hypothermia, hyperkalemia and dilutional/consumptive coagulopathy',[
 ('Guaranteed hypokalemia because stored cells contain no extracellular potassium','Potassium accumulates in stored-component supernatant.'),('Hyperthermia as the inevitable direct effect of cold blood','Unwarmed rapid transfusion can cause cooling.'),('A permanent rise in every coagulation factor despite ongoing bleeding','Loss, dilution and consumption can worsen coagulopathy.')],
 'Massive transfusion needs physiological and hemostatic monitoring', '**Cold components and rapid potassium delivery can cause important complications.**',['Shock and tissue injury add metabolic stress.','Ongoing hemorrhage can consume and dilute coagulation substrates.'], '**Use a coordinated major-hemorrhage protocol.**',['Warm appropriately and monitor electrolytes and coagulation.','Transition from empirical components to laboratory or viscoelastic guidance when suitable.'],[['Temperature','Hypothermia risk'],['Potassium','Hyperkalemia risk'],['Hemostasis','Loss, dilution and consumption']],[MA])
q('A bleeding patient has severe acquired hypofibrinogenemia. Which component is a concentrated fibrinogen source?', 'Cryoprecipitate, or an appropriate fibrinogen concentrate according to local availability and protocol',[
 ('Packed RBCs as the main fibrinogen replacement','They principally restore red-cell mass.'),('Albumin solution','It does not replace fibrinogen.'),('Platelets alone as an adequate fibrinogen replacement','They address a different hemostatic component.')],
 'Fibrinogen is a distinct substrate in clot formation', '**Cryoprecipitate contains concentrated fibrinogen.**',['It also contains selected other proteins, including VIII, XIII and VWF.','Modern specific concentrates are preferred for many inherited factor disorders when available.'], '**Replace according to active bleeding and measured hemostatic need.**',['Use local dose and target guidance.','Reassess fibrinogen and ongoing consumption.'],[['RBC','Oxygen-carrying cells'],['Platelets','Primary-hemostasis support'],['Cryoprecipitate','Concentrated fibrinogen']],[CR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('For ABO plasma compatibility, which donor group lacks anti-A and anti-B and can therefore supply plasma compatible with all ABO recipient groups?', 'Group AB',[
 ('Group O','O plasma contains anti-A and anti-B and is not universally compatible.'),('Group A','Its anti-B can react with B or AB recipient cells.'),('Group B','Its anti-A can react with A or AB recipient cells.')],
 'Plasma compatibility runs in the opposite direction from red-cell antigen compatibility', '**The relevant antibodies are in the donor plasma.**',['AB plasma lacks anti-A and anti-B.','Emergency low-titer group A plasma can be used under validated protocols.'], '**Do not transfer the universal red-cell donor rule to plasma.**',['Component-specific selection is required.','Use type-specific plasma when feasible.'],[['AB plasma','No anti-A or anti-B'],['O RBC','No A or B red-cell antigen'],['Emergency plasma','Protocol-defined alternatives may be used']],[CP])
q('A woman of childbearing potential has life-threatening bleeding and an unknown blood group. Which uncrossmatched RBC choice is generally preferred initially when available?', 'Group O RhD-negative RBCs while urgent typing and compatibility work proceed',[
 ('Group AB RhD-positive RBCs because AB is the universal RBC donor','AB red cells carry both A and B antigens.'),('Group A RBCs without a validated compatibility basis','Her ABO group is unknown.'),('Wait for complete testing despite immediately life-threatening hemorrhage','Emergency blood provision must not be inappropriately delayed.')],
 'Emergency provision balances immediate survival and alloimmunization risk', '**O red cells avoid A and B red-cell antigens.**',['RhD-negative supply is prioritized for patients with future pregnancy risk.','Local emergency policies account for inventory and patient characteristics.'], '**Obtain a properly identified pretransfusion specimen as soon as possible.**',['Switch to appropriately matched products when testing permits.','Unknown non-ABO antibodies can still create compatibility risk.'],[['Unknown ABO emergency','Group O RBC'],['Pregnancy potential','Prefer RhD negative'],['Follow-up','Urgent typing and compatible selection']],[CP,TE],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A previously documented clinically significant RBC alloantibody is no longer detectable on the current screen. What should the transfusion service do?', 'Honor the antibody history and select appropriately antigen-negative compatible RBCs',[
 ('Ignore the antibody because the current screen is negative','Evanescent antibodies can produce an anamnestic delayed reaction.'),('Use any ABO-compatible unit without considering the historical specificity','ABO compatibility does not address other clinically significant antigens.'),('Treat the historical antibody as an absolute prohibition against all future transfusion','Appropriate compatible support remains possible.')],
 'Antibody history persists even when measurable reactivity wanes', '**A negative current screen does not erase prior alloimmunization.**',['Re-exposure can trigger a rapid secondary response.','Accurate historical records improve component selection.'], '**Share transfusion and antibody records across institutions.**',['Allow extra time for antigen-negative unit procurement when possible.','In emergencies, transfusion medicine must balance compatibility and immediate need.'],[['Current screen','May be negative'],['History','Still clinically significant'],['Selection','Antigen-negative compatible units']],[TE])
q('Why should RhD-negative recipients with pregnancy potential receive RhD-compatible components whenever feasible?', 'Preventing anti-D alloimmunization reduces future hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn risk',[
 ('Anti-D is always naturally present in every RhD-negative person','It is generally an immune antibody following exposure.'),('RhD status determines ABO plasma antibodies','These are separate blood-group systems.'),('Platelets express large quantities of RhD themselves','Residual donor red cells, rather than platelet RhD expression, explain the relevant platelet-component exposure risk.')],
 'A transfusion today can affect a later pregnancy', '**RhD exposure can cause anti-D formation in a susceptible recipient.**',['IgG anti-D can cross the placenta in a later pregnancy.','RhD-negative platelet recipients may need prophylaxis in selected exposure circumstances.'], '**Use component-specific RhD policies and specialist advice.**',['Life-saving emergency transfusion remains the immediate priority.','Document exposure and appropriate follow-up.'],[['RhD exposure','Potential sensitization'],['Anti-D','Future fetal/neonatal hemolysis risk'],['Platelets','Residual RBC exposure can matter']],[TE,CP])

